        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.255,
            0.621,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 7.1"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.312,
            0.886,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Rank the following events on a scale from 0 (Impossible) to 1 (Certain). Label each event: Impossible, less likely, equally likely (even chance), more likely, certain. Give reasons why you gave each event its ranking."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.401,
            0.68,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) The next Monday will come after Sunday."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.428,
            0.567,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) It will snow in Mumbai in July."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.455,
            0.805,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) An elephant will walk through your classroom today."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.481,
            0.804,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) You will greet at least one friend at school tomorrow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.527,
            0.668,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.2 Measuring Probability Objectively"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.561,
            0.886,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are two main ways of estimating the probability of an event objectively:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.609,
            0.887,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using evidence from experience:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This involves collecting data either by performing an experiment multiple times or by analysing statistical data from past observations. In both cases, the probability is estimated by calculating the relative frequency of the event based on the collected evidence. We call this e"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "xperimental probability",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.74,
            0.888,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using theoretical methods:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This approach assumes that all "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "possible outcomes",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are equally likely (when there is no reason to believe that any one outcome is more likely than another). The probability is then determined by reasoning about the number of favourable outcomes in relation to the total number of possible outcomes. We call this "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "theoretical probability",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
